{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 1879018,
  "courseSlug": "nodejs-the-complete-guide",
  "courseTitle": "NodeJS - The Complete Guide",
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "20342133",
      "title": "Working with Type Aliases & Interfaces",
      "lectureOrder": 502,
      "videoOrder": 444,
      "sourceHash": "fbd001683fadeaa81d00c082b69fe13e6329da60e78b9e19c8cc7ac5cba18c56",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.12,
          "end": 3.97,
          "en": "Now in our TypeScript code,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 3.97,
          "end": 6.07,
          "en": "we have some repetition.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 6.07,
          "end": 8.55,
          "en": "Not horrible and definitely bearable,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 8.55,
          "end": 10.69,
          "en": "but we have some repetition and some code",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 10.69,
          "end": 12.3,
          "en": "that could be hard to read.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 12.3,
          "end": 14.61,
          "en": "For example here we have repetition,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 14.61,
          "end": 18.23,
          "en": "we re-use the same union type in two places.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 18.23,
          "end": 20.44,
          "en": "Now, this is definitely okay,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 20.44,
          "end": 22.14,
          "en": "but you could optimize this",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 22.14,
          "end": 24.91,
          "en": "or improve this with a type alias.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 24.91,
          "end": 28.15,
          "en": "TypeScript has a built in type operator,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 28.15,
          "end": 30.51,
          "en": "which does not exist in JavaScript,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 30.51,
          "end": 34.12,
          "en": "don't confuse it with type of, type is a different one.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 34.12,
          "end": 37.63,
          "en": "This allows you to set up your own type alias",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 37.63,
          "end": 40.37,
          "en": "so you can give a different type a new name.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 40.37,
          "end": 42.28,
          "en": "And that is especially useful",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 42.28,
          "end": 45.14,
          "en": "if you're working with union types for example.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 45.14,
          "end": 49.41,
          "en": "We can have our num or string type for example,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 49.41,
          "end": 52.04,
          "en": "the name is up to you, you could name it like this,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 52.04,
          "end": 55.49,
          "en": "and the value which we store in there is now a type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 55.49,
          "end": 57.52,
          "en": "So for example this union type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 57.52,
          "end": 61.7,
          "en": "And now we can use num or string in all the places",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 61.7,
          "end": 64.38,
          "en": "where we previously set up the union type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 64.38,
          "end": 69.28,
          "en": "And you can store anything, any type in such a type alias.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 69.28,
          "end": 73.78,
          "en": "Now if you compile this, if you compile this code,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 73.78,
          "end": 76.53,
          "en": "this will be gone, you don't see the type alias there",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 76.53,
          "end": 79.06,
          "en": "because it's a pure TypeScript feature.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 79.06,
          "end": 81.67,
          "en": "But it can save you some repetition.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 81.67,
          "end": 84.02,
          "en": "You can also use the type alias",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 84.02,
          "end": 86.91,
          "en": "to for example store your object type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 86.91,
          "end": 91.06,
          "en": "My result, and again that name is up to you,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 91.06,
          "end": 93.69,
          "en": "could be this object type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 93.69,
          "end": 96.28999999999999,
          "en": "So I can cut it from down there,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 96.28999999999999,
          "end": 98.793,
          "en": "and now use result down there.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 99.89,
          "end": 101.793,
          "en": "And now I'm referring to this object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 105.11,
          "end": 107.97999999999999,
          "en": "So type aliases can be very useful.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 107.97999999999999,
          "end": 111.03,
          "en": "An alternative, at least when you're working with",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 111.03,
          "end": 114.41,
          "en": "object types are interfaces.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 114.41,
          "end": 116.45,
          "en": "Interfaces also allow you",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 116.45,
          "end": 119.53,
          "en": "to define the structure of an object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 119.53,
          "end": 124.26,
          "en": "For example result object, to avoid a name clash.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 124.26,
          "end": 127.64,
          "en": "Here I could paste in that exact same structure",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 127.64,
          "end": 130.49,
          "en": "as for my result type alias.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 130.49,
          "end": 133.14,
          "en": "And I can now use result object",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 133.14,
          "end": 135.8,
          "en": "where I previously used result.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 135.8,
          "end": 137.91,
          "en": "Now why would you use an interface",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 137.91,
          "end": 140.81,
          "en": "or a type alias, what's the difference?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 140.81,
          "end": 143.32999999999998,
          "en": "If you're just defining the structure of an object,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 143.32999999999998,
          "end": 145.06,
          "en": "you can use either of the two.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 145.06,
          "end": 147.18,
          "en": "Using interfaces is a bit more common",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 147.18,
          "end": 148.82999999999998,
          "en": "but it's not a must to.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 148.82999999999998,
          "end": 153.47,
          "en": "Interfaces can however also be used to force classes",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 153.47,
          "end": 156.69,
          "en": "to implement certain methods or functionalities,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 156.69,
          "end": 159.91,
          "en": "but that's beyond the scope of this basics refresher,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 159.91,
          "end": 161.12,
          "en": "you do learn about it",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 161.12,
          "end": 164,
          "en": "in my understanding TypeScript course though.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 164,
          "end": 167.493,
          "en": "So for basic type aliasing, it doesn't matter.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 168.41,
          "end": 172.57,
          "en": "So, here we have a type alias, here we have the interface.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 172.57,
          "end": 174.78,
          "en": "A last important note is that",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 174.78,
          "end": 178.97,
          "en": "if you would add your own class or constructor function,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 178.97,
          "end": 182.06,
          "en": "you could use the class name as a type as well,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 182.06,
          "end": 184.06,
          "en": "just as we're using date here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 184.06,
          "end": 187.12,
          "en": "We can use date with new date",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 187.12,
          "end": 190.99,
          "en": "to instantiate it and get the current date time stamp,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 190.99,
          "end": 193.69,
          "en": "but we can also use it just as a type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 193.69,
          "end": 197.13,
          "en": "And that's true for any constructor function and class,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 197.13,
          "end": 200.123,
          "en": "no matter if it's a built in one, or your own one.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}
